{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"feature-screenplay-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Feature screenplay track","description":"Takes a feature film idea through logline, beat sheet, scene outline, first ten pages and a rewrite plan, stopping for the writer's approval at each step. Use when starting a feature screenplay.","category":"screenwriting","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","build","review"],"role":["writer"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","notes"],"output":["outline","script","plan"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["feature-film","screenplay-development","scene-outline","first-draft"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-logline-and-synopsis","write-beat-sheet","review-first-ten-pages","plan-rewrite-from-notes"],"personas":["script-consultant"]},"args":[{"name":"working_title","description":"Working title or a short name for the project.","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"idea","description":"The idea so far, in any form (a situation, a character, a genre, an image). Optional; step 1 asks for it if missing.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"concept","file":"steps/01-concept.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"script-notes/01-concept.md"},{"id":"beats","file":"steps/02-beats.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"script-notes/02-beat-sheet.md"},{"id":"outline","file":"steps/03-outline.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"script-notes/03-scene-outline.md"},{"id":"pages","file":"steps/04-pages.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"script-notes/04-first-ten-pages.md"},{"id":"rewrite","file":"steps/05-rewrite.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"script-notes/05-rewrite-plan.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Develops the feature \"{{working_title}}\"{{#idea}} (idea: {{idea}}){{/idea}} the way a development executive and a writer work before a first draft: a concept with a clear hook, a beat sheet that proves the structure, a scene outline the draft can follow, the first ten pages to test voice and opening, and a plan for the full draft and rewrite. Each step produces one document and stops for approval; later steps build on approved documents instead of re-asking.\n\nRules for every step: the writer owns the story, so offer options and ask for decisions on genre, protagonist, tone and ending instead of choosing silently; never contradict an approved step without flagging it; keep documents short enough to read in five minutes; one screenplay page is about a minute of screen time, and a feature usually runs 90 to 120 pages; write script pages only in step 4. If the writer wants to skip ahead, say in one line what each skipped step protects, offer a faster route (one question per step, shorter documents) and keep every approval gate.","steps":[{"id":"concept","file":"steps/01-concept.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"script-notes/01-concept.md","text":"# Step 1: Concept\n\nTurn the idea for \"{{working_title}}\" into a concept strong enough to structure.\n\n1. Ask, in one message, only what you cannot infer: the idea if none was given, genre and tone, the intended audience and budget level (micro, indie, studio), and anything that must stay (a character, a scene, an ending).\n2. Once answered, write three concept options. Each has a logline (protagonist with a flaw, inciting incident, goal, opposition, stakes), the central dramatic question, the hook that would make someone read it, and the closest genre expectations it must meet.\n3. For each option, name its biggest risk (passive protagonist, small stakes, familiar premise, budget beyond the level) in one line.\n4. Recommend one option, or a merge, and say why.\n\nWrite the document with sections Answers, Options, Recommendation.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to choose or adjust the concept."},{"id":"beats","file":"steps/02-beats.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"script-notes/02-beat-sheet.md","text":"# Step 2: Beat sheet\n\nBuild the structure for the approved concept.\n\n1. Write the story spine: protagonist's external goal, internal need, antagonist, stakes, theme as a question, and the ending.\n2. Build a beat sheet in a three-act or Save the Cat shape scaled to the target length, with page targets. For each beat, give the specific scene that delivers it and what changes, linked by cause (\"therefore\" or \"but\", never \"and then\").\n3. Describe the A and B stories and where they intersect.\n4. Check, marking OK or Flag: the protagonist makes the key choices at the act-one break, the midpoint and the climax; the midpoint changes the game; the all-is-lost moment costs something real; the climax is won by what the protagonist learned.\n\nWrite the document with sections Spine, Beat sheet, Storylines, Checks.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to approve or change the beats."},{"id":"outline","file":"steps/03-outline.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"script-notes/03-scene-outline.md","text":"# Step 3: Scene outline\n\nExpand the approved beat sheet into a scene-by-scene outline the draft can follow.\n\n1. List every scene in order with a slugline (INT. or EXT., place, time), who is in it, what the protagonist or point-of-view character wants in it, the turn, and an estimated page length.\n2. Mark the act breaks and the beats from step 2 on the scenes that deliver them.\n3. Flag scenes with no turn, two scenes doing the same job, and any stretch over ten pages where the protagonist's position does not change; propose a fix for each.\n4. Sum the page estimates and compare them with the target length.\n\nWrite the document with sections Scene outline, Flags, Length check.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to approve the outline."},{"id":"pages","file":"steps/04-pages.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"script-notes/04-first-ten-pages.md","text":"# Step 4: First ten pages\n\nDraft the opening to test voice, tone and how quickly the story grips.\n\n1. Write the first ten or so pages in standard screenplay format, following the approved outline: sluglines, lean action lines of one to three lines, character introductions in caps with an age and a telling action, and dialogue with subtext. No camera directions.\n2. By page ten the reader should know the protagonist, their want or flaw, the world, the tone and the genre promise, and feel the disruption coming.\n3. After the pages, add a short self-check: what the reader knows by page ten, the line or moment most likely to lose a reader, and two choices the writer might want to make differently.\n\nWrite the document with sections Pages, Self-check.\n\nStop and wait for the writer's reaction before planning the draft."},{"id":"rewrite","file":"steps/05-rewrite.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"script-notes/05-rewrite-plan.md","text":"# Step 5: Draft and rewrite plan\n\nPlan how the writer gets from approved pages to a draft worth sending out.\n\n1. Set a drafting schedule from the writer's available time: pages per session, milestones at the act breaks, and a rule to keep drafting forward and log fixes rather than rewriting mid-draft.\n2. List the open questions from steps 1 to 4 that the draft must answer, and where in the outline each lands.\n3. Plan the rewrite in passes (structure, character, scenes, polish) with what each pass checks.\n4. Plan feedback: when to get reads, from whom (peers, a table read), and what to ask them; then how to sort notes before changing anything.\n\nWrite the document with sections Schedule, Open questions, Rewrite passes, Feedback plan."}]}
